From 91b6c808a0a3a92d1581d447bb5cb99979a52a44 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Schaefer <stefanschaefer@BlackGuard.fritz.box>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 12:31:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Encrypted API access: optional HTTPS front door for the
 Console API, fingerprint in the installer and in Settings

A small container (runtime/tls-front, docker-compose.tls-front.yml) serves the
Console API over HTTPS with its own long-lived ECDSA key in front of the
unchanged Console, for tools that use API keys (for example MapViewer3D).
Clients pin the key; nothing else changes for anybody.

- API door by default: GET/HEAD /api/* and /images/maps/* only, Bearer dak_ key
  required, no cookies, no web UI, auth/settings/setup/discord routes blocked;
  rejected keys counted per client address (the Console only sees 127.0.0.1)
- installer: starts it on a new install (DUNE_ENCRYPTED_API=0 skips) and prints
  the address and the KEY FINGERPRINT next to the first admin password; the
  choice in runtime/generated/tls-front.env survives re-running the installer
- Settings -> Encrypted API Access: status, switch, address, key fingerprint with
  Copy (GET/POST /api/settings/encrypted-api, settings:read/write); the Console
  reads only the certificate, never the private key
- dune encrypted-api enable|disable|status|fingerprint (runtime/scripts/tls-front.sh)
- tests: encryptedApi.test.js (12, real certificate, pin checked with openssl),
  tls-front-script-test.sh (docker mock), EncryptedApiSection.test.tsx (4),
  Go tests of the front door; CI builds the image
- docs/console/encrypted-api.md, README port table, docs index

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
